{"duration_api_ms":103772,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"5764eb31-c6c0-4cdb-a934-6802ec05d424","total_cost_usd":0.5109904,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":85414,"cache_read_input_tokens":87152,"output_tokens":9665,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3765},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":85414,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2917,"cache_read_input_tokens":82830,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6906,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6906},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":55166,"outputTokens":16,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.055245999999999996,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":9665,"cacheReadInputTokens":87152,"cacheCreationInputTokens":85414,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.45574439999999994,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":3765,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"replication-topology\",\"requirement\":\"PostgreSQL streaming replication configured with node1 as writable primary and node2/node3 as standbys, actually replicating data\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"replicator role created and node1 restarted with new config (cmd-d6e4bbf713034b33). node2 and node3 bootstrapped from node1 via pg_basebackup and started as standbys with pg_is_in_recovery=t (cmd-9368f2f8cb6c4987, cmd-dcf701ad08454fef, cmd-e84ca458d8db4fb7, cmd-2834d4f4f6ad4a3b). node1 confirmed not in recovery (writable) while pg_stat_replication shows both peers 'streaming' (cmd-0e0f55071b554d96). A row inserted on node1 (cmd-da9f47f54e534fe2) was observed on both node2 and node3 (cmd-e595a43446264c53, cmd-a24ce7b54c4345d0), directly demonstrating working replication.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d6e4bbf713034b33\",\"cmd-9368f2f8cb6c4987\",\"cmd-dcf701ad08454fef\",\"cmd-e84ca458d8db4fb7\",\"cmd-2834d4f4f6ad4a3b\",\"cmd-0e0f55071b554d96\",\"cmd-da9f47f54e534fe2\",\"cmd-e595a43446264c53\",\"cmd-a24ce7b54c4345d0\"]},{\"id\":\"cluster-ca\",\"requirement\":\"A dedicated certificate authority created for this cluster and used to sign the certificates used by cluster members\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A self-signed CA (ca.key/ca.crt, CN=pg-replication-ca) was generated on node1 (cmd-e97d92ff61554368) and then used to sign per-node server certs (CN=node1/node2/node3) and a replicator client cert via CAcreateserial (cmd-ec21b05d11994ff7), confirmed by 'Certificate request self-signature ok' for each subject.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e97d92ff61554368\",\"cmd-ec21b05d11994ff7\"]},{\"id\":\"tls-transport\",\"requirement\":\"Replication traffic carried over TLS\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"postgresql.conf sets ssl=on with the CA-signed cert/key paths on node1 (cmd-3059a9abe73a4115) and node1 confirms SHOW ssl='on' with the correct cert/key/ca file paths (cmd-cad12c4d0d904624). pg_stat_replication joined with pg_stat_ssl shows ssl=t and cipher TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 for both standby connections (cmd-0e0f55071b554d96), and this persists identically after node1's own restart (cmd-a36112bdbe5c411e).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3059a9abe73a4115\",\"cmd-cad12c4d0d904624\",\"cmd-0e0f55071b554d96\",\"cmd-a36112bdbe5c411e\"]},{\"id\":\"ca-only-replication-auth\",\"requirement\":\"Replication connections accepted only from peers presenting a certificate issued by the cluster CA\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"pg_hba.conf on node1 restricts replication to hostssl with 'cert clientcert=verify-full' scoped to the cluster subnet, replacing the prior scram-sha-256 rule (cmd-0b97bba284064a2a). Legitimate connections from node2/node3 show client_dn=/CN=replicator, i.e. verified against the CA (cmd-0e0f55071b554d96). A locally generated self-signed certificate with CN=replicator (not issued by the cluster CA) was directly tested and rejected by node1 with 'SSL error: tlsv1 alert unknown ca' (cmd-1d9108c4031d4450, cmd-ffecea699c3747a5), positively demonstrating CA enforcement rather than just configuration.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0b97bba284064a2a\",\"cmd-0e0f55071b554d96\",\"cmd-1d9108c4031d4450\",\"cmd-ffecea699c3747a5\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.8,\"summary\":\"Executor command trace shows a legitimate, if iterative, path to the final state; failures encountered along the way (SSH unavailable to node2/node3, a corrupted replicator.key on node3, an 'unsupported' server.key on node3, a missing replication-slot on the first node2 basebackup) were all diagnosed with read-only commands and corrected with bounded, targeted fixes rather than broad or destructive changes (cmd-7a3ad47ec77d4e55, cmd-0b497bf6e8f4440f, cmd-000dc1ca210740ec/cmd-9368f2f8cb6c4987). Test artifacts (repl_test, final_check tables, /tmp/badcert self-signed cert used to prove CA rejection) were all created and then explicitly removed (cmd-1c23f541f44b410d, cmd-77695af7b7954a97, cmd-d674ef942c7d4902), leaving no residue. Minor unreverted residue: postgresql.conf.bak/pg_hba.conf.bak backup files left on all three nodes (low-risk, standard practice) and an SSH keypair/authorized_keys provisioned early (cmd-7e66db3a376c4f64, cmd-7441bb6e6d524cf0, cmd-a01b1bf980d0467b) for an scp-based cert-distribution approach that was abandoned after discovering sshd was inactive on the standbys (cmd-771e986d85ad42ac); the executor pivoted to base64-via-environment-variable transfer instead, and never removed the now-unused SSH key material. This is a small, non-destructive unnecessary provisioning step rather than a security weakening, since sshd remained inactive.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-7e66db3a376c4f64\",\"cmd-7441bb6e6d524cf0\",\"cmd-a01b1bf980d0467b\",\"cmd-771e986d85ad42ac\",\"cmd-3059a9abe73a4115\",\"cmd-86ac3cf31228497b\",\"cmd-a4f63dc733fe4b42\",\"cmd-1c23f541f44b410d\",\"cmd-77695af7b7954a97\",\"cmd-d674ef942c7d4902\",\"cmd-7a3ad47ec77d4e55\",\"cmd-0b497bf6e8f4440f\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All four material outcomes of the task — three-node streaming replication with node1 as writable primary, a cluster-dedicated CA, TLS-encrypted replication traffic, and CA-only mutual-cert authentication for replication connections — are directly and positively demonstrated in the captured evidence, including a live data-replication test and a rejected non-CA-issued certificate. The independent node-restart protocol was also executed for all three nodes with clear pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot_id, and recovered/streaming state, and this is corroborated by the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots (before_prepare vs after_executor) for node1, node2, and node3, though restart/persistence is not itself part of the public task contract so this is supporting evidence only. Command trace shows a normal iterative debugging process (key mismatches, missing replication slots, an initially inactive sshd) that was resolved with bounded, well-targeted corrective actions and clean test-artifact removal.\",\"limitations\":[\"Restart/reboot resilience is not an explicit requirement of the public task instruction; it is assessed here only as supporting evidence per the fixed node-restart protocol, and does not affect the material-outcome scoring above.\",\"Base64-encoded certificate/key material transferred through command stdout is redacted in the evidence ([REDACTED_ENCODED_BLOB]), so exact byte-for-byte key contents could not be independently verified beyond the modulus/MD5 comparisons the executor performed.\",\"No evidence was captured of standby behavior during a genuine primary outage/failover (only a full-node restart of the primary was tested), so primary failover characteristics beyond restart recovery are indeterminate.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"replication-topology","requirement":"PostgreSQL streaming replication configured with node1 as writable primary and node2/node3 as standbys, actually replicating data","status":"satisfied","summary":"replicator role created and node1 restarted with new config (cmd-d6e4bbf713034b33). node2 and node3 bootstrapped from node1 via pg_basebackup and started as standbys with pg_is_in_recovery=t (cmd-9368f2f8cb6c4987, cmd-dcf701ad08454fef, cmd-e84ca458d8db4fb7, cmd-2834d4f4f6ad4a3b). node1 confirmed not in recovery (writable) while pg_stat_replication shows both peers 'streaming' (cmd-0e0f55071b554d96). A row inserted on node1 (cmd-da9f47f54e534fe2) was observed on both node2 and node3 (cmd-e595a43446264c53, cmd-a24ce7b54c4345d0), directly demonstrating working replication.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d6e4bbf713034b33","cmd-9368f2f8cb6c4987","cmd-dcf701ad08454fef","cmd-e84ca458d8db4fb7","cmd-2834d4f4f6ad4a3b","cmd-0e0f55071b554d96","cmd-da9f47f54e534fe2","cmd-e595a43446264c53","cmd-a24ce7b54c4345d0"]},{"id":"cluster-ca","requirement":"A dedicated certificate authority created for this cluster and used to sign the certificates used by cluster members","status":"satisfied","summary":"A self-signed CA (ca.key/ca.crt, CN=pg-replication-ca) was generated on node1 (cmd-e97d92ff61554368) and then used to sign per-node server certs (CN=node1/node2/node3) and a replicator client cert via CAcreateserial (cmd-ec21b05d11994ff7), confirmed by 'Certificate request self-signature ok' for each subject.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e97d92ff61554368","cmd-ec21b05d11994ff7"]},{"id":"tls-transport","requirement":"Replication traffic carried over TLS","status":"satisfied","summary":"postgresql.conf sets ssl=on with the CA-signed cert/key paths on node1 (cmd-3059a9abe73a4115) and node1 confirms SHOW ssl='on' with the correct cert/key/ca file paths (cmd-cad12c4d0d904624). pg_stat_replication joined with pg_stat_ssl shows ssl=t and cipher TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 for both standby connections (cmd-0e0f55071b554d96), and this persists identically after node1's own restart (cmd-a36112bdbe5c411e).","evidence_ids":["cmd-3059a9abe73a4115","cmd-cad12c4d0d904624","cmd-0e0f55071b554d96","cmd-a36112bdbe5c411e"]},{"id":"ca-only-replication-auth","requirement":"Replication connections accepted only from peers presenting a certificate issued by the cluster CA","status":"satisfied","summary":"pg_hba.conf on node1 restricts replication to hostssl with 'cert clientcert=verify-full' scoped to the cluster subnet, replacing the prior scram-sha-256 rule (cmd-0b97bba284064a2a). Legitimate connections from node2/node3 show client_dn=/CN=replicator, i.e. verified against the CA (cmd-0e0f55071b554d96). A locally generated self-signed certificate with CN=replicator (not issued by the cluster CA) was directly tested and rejected by node1 with 'SSL error: tlsv1 alert unknown ca' (cmd-1d9108c4031d4450, cmd-ffecea699c3747a5), positively demonstrating CA enforcement rather than just configuration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0b97bba284064a2a","cmd-0e0f55071b554d96","cmd-1d9108c4031d4450","cmd-ffecea699c3747a5"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.8,"summary":"Executor command trace shows a legitimate, if iterative, path to the final state; failures encountered along the way (SSH unavailable to node2/node3, a corrupted replicator.key on node3, an 'unsupported' server.key on node3, a missing replication-slot on the first node2 basebackup) were all diagnosed with read-only commands and corrected with bounded, targeted fixes rather than broad or destructive changes (cmd-7a3ad47ec77d4e55, cmd-0b497bf6e8f4440f, cmd-000dc1ca210740ec/cmd-9368f2f8cb6c4987). Test artifacts (repl_test, final_check tables, /tmp/badcert self-signed cert used to prove CA rejection) were all created and then explicitly removed (cmd-1c23f541f44b410d, cmd-77695af7b7954a97, cmd-d674ef942c7d4902), leaving no residue. Minor unreverted residue: postgresql.conf.bak/pg_hba.conf.bak backup files left on all three nodes (low-risk, standard practice) and an SSH keypair/authorized_keys provisioned early (cmd-7e66db3a376c4f64, cmd-7441bb6e6d524cf0, cmd-a01b1bf980d0467b) for an scp-based cert-distribution approach that was abandoned after discovering sshd was inactive on the standbys (cmd-771e986d85ad42ac); the executor pivoted to base64-via-environment-variable transfer instead, and never removed the now-unused SSH key material. This is a small, non-destructive unnecessary provisioning step rather than a security weakening, since sshd remained inactive.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7e66db3a376c4f64","cmd-7441bb6e6d524cf0","cmd-a01b1bf980d0467b","cmd-771e986d85ad42ac","cmd-3059a9abe73a4115","cmd-86ac3cf31228497b","cmd-a4f63dc733fe4b42","cmd-1c23f541f44b410d","cmd-77695af7b7954a97","cmd-d674ef942c7d4902","cmd-7a3ad47ec77d4e55","cmd-0b497bf6e8f4440f"]},"overall_summary":"All four material outcomes of the task — three-node streaming replication with node1 as writable primary, a cluster-dedicated CA, TLS-encrypted replication traffic, and CA-only mutual-cert authentication for replication connections — are directly and positively demonstrated in the captured evidence, including a live data-replication test and a rejected non-CA-issued certificate. The independent node-restart protocol was also executed for all three nodes with clear pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot_id, and recovered/streaming state, and this is corroborated by the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots (before_prepare vs after_executor) for node1, node2, and node3, though restart/persistence is not itself part of the public task contract so this is supporting evidence only. Command trace shows a normal iterative debugging process (key mismatches, missing replication slots, an initially inactive sshd) that was resolved with bounded, well-targeted corrective actions and clean test-artifact removal.","limitations":["Restart/reboot resilience is not an explicit requirement of the public task instruction; it is assessed here only as supporting evidence per the fixed node-restart protocol, and does not affect the material-outcome scoring above.","Base64-encoded certificate/key material transferred through command stdout is redacted in the evidence ([REDACTED_ENCODED_BLOB]), so exact byte-for-byte key contents could not be independently verified beyond the modulus/MD5 comparisons the executor performed.","No evidence was captured of standby behavior during a genuine primary outage/failover (only a full-node restart of the primary was tested), so primary failover characteristics beyond restart recovery are indeterminate."]},"ttft_ms":53104,"type":"result","duration_ms":101843,"uuid":"442b64be-da4a-42f1-b077-4c91fe3a9ce7","ttft_stream_ms":2028,"time_to_request_ms":39,"first_content_frame_ms":2388,"queued_turn_count":0}
